// How a country is arranged: who decides, and by what right.
//
// A form of government is not an ideology, and the two are separate on purpose. A
// parliamentary republic can be liberal or socialist; a one-party state can be socialist or
// fascist; an absolute monarchy has an ideology whether or not it admits to one. Fold the
// two together and you get a list of twenty things that are half of each, with nowhere to
// put a conservative constitutional monarchy.
//
// `centralisation` runs from a federation of provinces at 0 to one office at 1. A
// centralised state decides faster and is resented more; that is the whole trade, and it is
// one number, so a new government is a matter of choosing where on it to sit.
//
// `change_cost` multiplies what walking away from this arrangement costs in stability.
// Leaving a parliamentary republic is an election. Leaving an absolute monarchy is a
// different kind of afternoon.
//
// `effects` are weekly, except `growth`, which is annual because growth is quoted annually:
//     growth · stability · war_support · radicalism · living · literacy
//
// The first entry is what a country is when nothing has said otherwise.

{
  "parliamentary_republic": {
    "name": "Parliamentary republic",
    "description": "A head of government who holds office only while a chamber lets him. Slow to turn, hard to capture, and very difficult to make do anything unpopular in a hurry.",
    "order": 10,
    "centralisation": 0.42,
    "change_cost": 0.8,
    "militia": 1.05,
    "effects": { "stability": 0.00010, "living": 0.00004 }
  },

  "presidential_republic": {
